  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "The Stanford marshmallow ( (棉花糖) ) test was {originally} conducted by psychologist Walter Mischel in the late 1960s.This experiment has {forever} changed our understanding of self-control and delayed gratification.Children aged four to six at a {nursery} school were placed in a room.A single {sugary} treat was placed on a table.Each child was told if they waited for 15 minutes before eating the treat, they would be given a second treat.The researcher {delivered} this message and then {exited} , leaving the children alone in the room.Some children {folded} immediately under pressure, while others showed {resistance} to the temptation.Years later, when researchers {inquired} about the participants' life outcomes, there was a connection between the ability to {postpone} gratification and various forms of success.",
      "cn": "斯坦福棉花糖实验 最初 是由心理学家沃尔特·米谢尔在20世纪60年代末进行的。这个实验 永远 改变了我们对自我控制和延迟满足的认识。在一所 幼儿园 里，年龄在4岁到6岁的孩子被安排在一个房间里。桌上放着一块 甜甜的 糖果。每个孩子都被告知，如果他们在吃这块糖果之前等待15分钟，就会得到第二块糖果。研究人员 传达 完信息后就 离开 了，把孩子们单独留在房间里。一些孩子在压力之下马上就 妥协 了，而另一些孩子则对诱惑表现出了 抵抗力 。多年后，当研究人员 询问 参与者的人生成就时发现， 延迟 满足的能力与各种形式的成功之间存在着某种联系。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "As adults, we face a {version} of the marshmallow test every day.We're not tempted ( (诱惑) ) by sugary treats, but by our computers, phones, and {tablets} —all the devices that connect us to the global {delivery} system for various types of information that do to us what marshmallows do to {preschoolers} .We often find ourselves {wrestling} with distractions.",
      "cn": "作为成年人，我们每天都要面对另一种 形式 的棉花糖实验。诱惑我们的并非甜甜的糖果，而是电脑、手机和 平板电脑 ——所有这些将我们与各类信息的全球 传递 系统相连接的设备，这些信息对我们造成的影响就像棉花糖对 学龄前儿童 的影响一样。我们经常发现自己在与分散注意力的事物相 抗争 。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "We are tempted by sugary treats.One reason is that sweet {flavors} often indicated ripe fruits like {apples} or {grapes} that were safe to eat, while bitter tastes could signal {poison} .It is not just about safety.Our {ancestors} lived in a calorie-poor world. {Fats} and sugars were both crucial for survival, but the latter offered a quick source of energy, which could have distinguished between survival and {death} .Therefore, our brains developed a response {mechanism} to sugary treats that reflected their value—a feeling of reward and satisfaction.But as we've {reshaped} the world around us, sugary {desserts} and {delicious} treats became readily available, and we no longer needed to hunt {lambs} , which {dramatically} reduced the {cost} and effort {involved} in obtaining calories.Yet we still have the same brains we had {thousands} of years ago, and this {mismatch} is at the heart of why so many of us struggle to {resist tempting} foods that we know we shouldn't eat.",
      "cn": "我们会受到甜食的诱惑。其中一个原因在于，甜 味 通常暗示了 苹果 、 葡萄 等可以安全食用的成熟水果，而苦味则可能是有 毒 的标志。这不仅仅是安全的问题。我们的 祖先 生活在一个热量匮乏的世界。 脂肪 和糖对于生存而言都是至关重要的，但后者能迅速提供能量，它在当时可能决定了生 死 存 亡 。因此，我们的大脑对甜食形成了一种反应 机制 ，这种机制反映了甜食的价值——一种受到奖赏的、满足的感受。但是，随着我们对周遭世界的 改造 ，含糖的 甜点 和 美味的 食品变得唾手可得，我们也不再需要捕猎 羔羊 ，这 大大 降低了获取热量所 涉及的成本 和精力。然而，我们的大脑仍然和 几千 年前一样，而这种 不匹配 正是我们当中这么多人难以 抵抗 那些我们明知不该吃的、 诱人的 食物的核心原因。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "A similar process is at work in our response to information.Our {formative environment} as a {species} was information-poor, so our brains developed a mechanism that {prized} new information.But global {connectivity} has greatly changed our information environment.We are now {ceaselessly} bombarded ( (轰炸) ) with new information.With a simple click on the {keyboard} , we can {download} an overwhelming amount of data.We {surf} the internet, {subscribe} to countless newsletters, and our inboxes fill up with {mail} , making it increasingly difficult to {guard} our attention.The digital cloud is not just a place where data is stored; it has made our minds {cloudy} , too.Therefore, just as we need to be more {thoughtful} about our {caloric consumption} , we must learn to {swim} through the ocean of information without sinking into a {soup} of meaningless data, resisting the {temptation} of the mental“ {junk} food” in order to manage our time most {effectively} .",
      "cn": "类似的过程也在我们对信息的反应中发挥作用。作为一个 物种 ，我们 形成 期所处的 环境 是信息匮乏的，因此我们的大脑形成了一种 重视 新信息的机制。但全球的 互联互通 极大地改变了我们的信息环境。现在，我们 不停地 受到新信息的轰炸。只需简单地敲击一下 键盘 ，我们就能 下载 大量的数据。我们在互联网上 冲浪 ， 订阅 无数的时事通讯，收件箱被 邮件 塞得满满的，这使得我们越来越难以 维系 我们的注意力。数字“云”不仅是一个存储数据的地方，还让我们的头脑也变得“ 云里雾里 ”。因此，正如我们需要对 热量的摄入 多加 思考 一样，我们必须学会在信息的海洋中畅 游 ，而不会沉没在毫无意义的数据 洪流 中，我们要抵制精神上的“ 垃圾 食品”的 诱惑 ，以便最 有效地 管理我们的时间。 5NoOncib/NjiX02BpMIzP4m+378Hw1xTRxUdPaP/PFjKUFq4K2G7tp6ZLYoCE601yW1W39VMdLmovFdSHs4nVQ=="
    }
  ],
